A number can be divided by 2 to remove its last binary digit. Before dividing, `n % 2` tells whether that last digit is `1`.

The loop also works for zero. If `n` is `0`, it never enters the loop and returns `0`.

```java
class Solution {
    public int countSetBits(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        while (n > 0) {
            count += n % 2;
            n /= 2;
        }
        return count;
    }
}
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

Kernighan's trick removes one set bit on every loop. The operation `n & (n - 1)` changes the last `1` bit into `0`, so the number of loops is exactly the number of set bits.

Dry run for `n = 13` (`1101`):
- `1101 -> 1100`, count 1
- `1100 -> 1000`, count 2
- `1000 -> 0000`, count 3

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countSetBits(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        while (n != 0) {
            n = n & (n - 1);
            count++;
        }
        return count;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(k)`, where `k` is the number of set bits.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.

This version turns the number into its binary form and counts the characters equal to `'1'`. It is easy to check and does not need any manual bit operations.

For example, `10` becomes `1010`, so the answer is `2`.

```python
class Solution:
    def countSetBits(self, n: int) -> int:
        return bin(n).count("1")
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`, because the binary representation has that many digits.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` for the binary string.

The lowest bit is `n % 2`, and the remaining bits are found in `n // 2`. Adding the lowest bit to the result of the smaller number gives the total count.

Dry run for `n = 7`:
`7 % 2 = 1`, then `3`, `1`, and `0` each add one more. The final count is `3`.

The important edge case is `n = 0`, which is the stopping condition. This code does not modify the input value.

```python
class Solution:
    def countSetBits(self, n: int) -> int:
        if n == 0:
            return 0
        return (n % 2) + self.countSetBits(n // 2)
```

Time complexity: `O(log n)`.
Space complexity: `O(log n)` because of the recursive call stack.

Here the mask visits each bit position from right to left. If `(n & mask)` is not zero, that position contains a set bit.

This checks all 32 positions, so the running time stays constant for a normal 32-bit integer. The input value is not changed.

Edge cases such as `n = 0` return `0`, while `n = 1` returns `1`.

```cpp
class Solution {
public:
    int countSetBits(int n) {
        int count = 0;
        unsigned int mask = 1;

        for (int bit = 0; bit < 32; bit++) {
            if ((static_cast<unsigned int>(n) & mask) != 0) {
                count++;
            }
            mask <<= 1;
        }

        return count;
    }
};
```

Time complexity: `O(32)`, which is `O(1)` for 32-bit integers.
Space complexity: `O(1)`.
